---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Prose from "../components/Prose.astro";
---
<BaseLayout title="About · Sundharesan Kumaresan">
  <main id="main-content" class="mx-auto max-w-[1100px] px-6 pt-16 pb-8">
    <header data-reveal class="border-t border-border pt-5">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
        <span class="font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-text-muted">The long version</span>
        <span class="font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-accent">AI engineer &middot; writer</span>
      </div>
    </header>
    <div data-reveal class="mt-8 grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-[0.95fr_1.05fr] md:gap-14">
      <div>
        <h1 class="font-serif font-semibold leading-[0.95]" style="font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5.5vw, 3.6rem);">Sundharesan Kumaresan</h1>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-[42ch] text-lg text-text-muted">An AI engineer and writer working one question from two sides: <span class="text-text">fairness and legibility, who a system notices and who it never has to.</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="aspect-[654/801] min-h-[420px] w-full max-w-[460px] bg-contain bg-center bg-no-repeat md:order-first md:max-w-[520px] md:bg-fixed" style="background-image:url('/img/hero-crop.jpeg')" role="img" aria-label="Portrait of Sundharesan Kumaresan"></div>
    </div>
    <div data-reveal class="mt-16"><Prose dropcap>
      <p>People expect a portfolio to pick a lane. Engineer or writer. Builder or storyteller. I've never managed it, and I've stopped apologising for it. They're the same job done with different tools: take something tangled and human, and make it legible enough to act on. Code does that for machines. Stories do it for people. I do both because I can't help it.</p>
      <p>By day I'm an AI engineer at Oogway Labs, which is a tidy title for a messy job. Someone arrives with a problem and no plan, and I'm the one who sits with them, sketches the fix, proves it isn't just optimism, and builds the thing end to end. Mostly AI. I'm an early adopter to a fault: I'll trust a new model exactly as far as I've personally tried to break it. I treat every build like a story, a problem, some tension, a third act where it finally clicks, and my job is getting us there without the scene where everything's on fire.</p>
      <p>Lately I've been reading my way into psychology too. Two reasons. The professional one: every product that actually works is sitting on top of how people think, and pretending otherwise gets you software no one uses. The other is plainer. People are the strangest, most interesting thing I've ever tried to understand, and I'd like to be a little less wrong about us by the end of it.</p>
      <p>At night I write fiction. Not as a hobby that photographs well, but because it's the only way I've found to work out what I actually think. My stories keep circling the same nerve: the distance between what a situation looks like and what's true inside it, and how often a whole life lives in that gap. I write economics and the odd technical piece too, but the fiction is where I'm most honest, probably because nobody's grading it.</p>
      <p>Underneath all of it sits one question I can't put down: who a system is built to count, and who it quietly doesn't. I read my way around it, economists who treat the poor as people making rational decisions rather than numbers, novelists who sit with the ones history forgot. It isn't abstract. The people who grow our food, the kid in a small town who could medal for the country, they're invisible to the systems that could change their lives. That invisibility is a choice someone made, usually by accident, always with consequences.</p>
      <p>That question is also where I'm headed, eventually. I haven't built any of it yet, and I'm not going to pretend otherwise. Right now I'm doing the unglamorous part: learning the economics and the logistics, getting good enough as an engineer that I could actually pull off the thing I have in mind. When I'm ready I'll know roughly who it's for. I keep that aim under To, including the parts I still have to prove to myself.</p>
      <p>There's also a thing I haven't done yet but quietly want to: model. No shoots, no catalogue, not one proper photo, just a hunch I'd enjoy being in front of a camera and a plan to find out if the chance comes. It's the same impulse as everything else, really: try the thing, keep what survives. The code, the fiction, the plans I'm not ready for yet, it's all one story told in different registers. The conviction under all of it is simple, even if living up to it isn't.</p>
    </Prose></div>
    <blockquote data-reveal class="mt-24 max-w-[20ch] border-l-2 border-stellar pl-8 font-serif font-semibold leading-[1.02]" style="font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 5.5vw, 3.6rem);">
      &ldquo;Who a system sees is a moral choice.&rdquo;
    </blockquote>
    <div data-reveal class="mt-16 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm">
      <a href="mailto:sundharesansk11@gmail.com" class="u-underline text-text hover:text-accent">Email</a>
      <a href="https://x.com/sundharesansk11" class="u-underline text-text hover:text-accent">X</a>
      <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sundharesan-kumaresan-iyal11" class="u-underline text-text hover:text-accent">LinkedIn</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/sundharesan11" class="u-underline text-text hover:text-accent">GitHub</a>
      <a href="https://medium.com/@sundharesansk11" class="u-underline text-text hover:text-accent">Medium</a>
      <a href="https://www.instagram.com/sundharesan_11/" class="u-underline text-text hover:text-accent">Instagram</a>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
